dimension is when you look at direct  variation these are equations in this  form y equals K times X sometimes we'll  write them y equals a times X but this K  value or a value is what they call the  constant of variation it's what relates  the two variables now with inverse  variation these are equations that are  in the form y equals a divided by ax or  y equals K divided by ax depending on  what letter your book or your class your  teacher uses to denote the constant of  variation but the thing to notice here  is that the ax here is in the numerator  and with inverse variation the X here is  in the denominator now what we're going  to do is we're going to rearrange the  equation to solve for the constant of  variation so let's just work with the a  here so if we solve for a we're going to  divide both sides by ax and so you can  see that a equals Y divided by X what  this means is that if we take all the Y  values divided by the X values and we  get the same a value each time the same  constant that tells us that this is  direct variation and it's going to be y  equals a times X okay now here if we  solve for a constant of variation let's  just use this one up here y equals a  divided by X if we multiply both sides  by ax you can see that a is going to  equal x times y so if we multiply the X  and y values together and we keep  getting the same a value then that tells  us that it's inverse variation so let's  see if I can show you what I mean in  these two examples so first thing we're  going to do here is let's see if we  multiply if we multiply the X and Y's  together you see how we get 3 12 3 times  9 is 27 4 times 12 is 48 we're not  getting the same quantity each time so  that tells us that it's not going to be  an inverse variation equation  let's try dividing so we're going to  take Y divided by X so 3 divided by 1 is  3 6 divided by 2 is 3 9 divided by 3 is  3 12 divided by 4 is 3 we're getting the  same a value or constant of variation so  that tells us 